    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a7ce580c_bd6e54b3",
        "filename": "app/src/main/java/org/opencord/olt/impl/Olt.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000003
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-17T17:51:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My point really is, that data from SADIS should only be read \"as needed\". So I am not even sure that data should be copied from SADIS to OLT data. Instead, when the OLT app needs data, that is when it should query SADIS.",
      "parentUuid": "1c0ab46c_4688fdfd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 193,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 193,
        "endChar": 76
      },
      "revId": "8b813c2ef84d4b11cb9a7acb4fe3512204b46a75",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d2d54ac9_2c89d7dc",
        "filename": "app/src/main/java/org/opencord/olt/impl/Olt.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000097
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-18T04:28:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes David, I understand your point. Couple of points\n\n1. We are only really copying the S-tag for the OLT from Sadis, the ports are something that we already have from the device but Sadis configuration tells us which out of them is the uplink port. I am not in favour of associating a S-tag with an OLT, but this is something that RCORD did.\n\n2. This change is backward compatible, in the sense that you could still provision the OLT data using the ONOS configuration (netcfg). If it finds the OLT data in the ONOS configuration it stores it in local data structure (oltData) else it looks at Sadis. So the copying comes from that. All the code assumes that OLTs data to be present in \"oltData\". We can get rid of copying the data from Sadis completely if we remove this backward compatibility (there would be good amount of churn in the code).\n\nMy suggestion is that we take this patchset as an intermediate step to go towards that; i.e. remove association of an OLT with a particular S-tag and also have provisioning of OLTs in ONOS only using Sadis.",
      "parentUuid": "a7ce580c_bd6e54b3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 193,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 193,
        "endChar": 76
      },
      "revId": "8b813c2ef84d4b11cb9a7acb4fe3512204b46a75",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "60940347_f9e7f7b3",
        "filename": "app/src/main/java/org/opencord/olt/impl/Olt.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000003
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-18T18:34:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is there a requirement to stay backward compatible? Why not move all data integration into SADIS and kick the major version number of the olt app to the next number? As SADIS can use data in netcfg it can still be done this way, well slightly different, and in a real deployment, the data will be available via the integration interface.\n\nMy concern is that things will start to like like a patch-work if we prioritize back compatability over forward driection, or things we plan to fix later just end up as a pile of technical debt and either never get addressed or we continue to make decisions for \"time\" issues and maintain the debt.",
      "parentUuid": "d2d54ac9_2c89d7dc",
